Post Date: 2020-10-12T21:17:59Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: [QUESTION] How do you DeleteMany efficiently
Description: I've looked through the documentation and there is a lot of information about querying and indexes to query things efficiently, but I don't see a lot of information about deleting things efficiently.

I'm trying to DeleteMany from a LiteDb database, but even relatively small deletes of 3000 items takes 10-20 seconds. Right now I am just experimenting with smaller data sets, but I plan to scale things up to much larger data sets, but I'm concerned that if the performance scales as well, then some of my deletion operations may take 30 minutes or more.

I'm trying to make a generic database implementation using LiteDB, and below is the code that I'm using for deleting from the database

```csharp
public void Delete<T>(IEnumerable<T> items)
{
    //Gets the collection name based on type. Will throw an exception if we don't support the type used.
    var collectionName = getCollectionNameAndThrowIfNotSupported<T>();
    //Gets the connection string. We are only specifying Filename, ReadOnly, and the Connection is shared
    var connectionString = getConnectionString(read_only: false);
    using (var db = new LiteDatabase(connectionString))
    {
        var collectionExists = db.CollectionExists(collectionName);
        if (!collectionExists)
            return; //no collection exists, so there is nothing to delete

        //converting items to a hash set to help find matches more quickly
        var hashedItems = items.ToHashSet();

        var collection = db.GetCollection<T>(collectionName);
        //This is the line that I have the most questions about. Is there a better way of writing this line to be more efficient?
        collection.DeleteMany(x => hashedItems.Contains(x));
    }
}
``` 

I thought that `Contains` would check if the items are equal, and for my objects that are being stored in LiteDb I am overriding `Equals` and `GetHashCode` and for two items to be equal I am only checking if the `ObjectId`s are equal.

From the documentation it would appear that if you have an `ObjectId Id` that this will automatically get indexed, but am I missing something in my `DeleteMany` predicate that would help take advantage of indexes? Is there something that can be done to make this process faster?

